From f41e647295767be667e1a969b505e67f345ff095 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ruscher <rruscher@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 20:06:33 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] wmagnify: fast rendering, mouse wheel zoom, smooth scaling,
 free resizing

The magnifier refreshed every 200 ms and drew each magnified pixel as a
rectangle (XGetPixel on the captured image, one XFillRectangles batch
per run of equal pixels), so it showed about 4 frames per second while
keeping both the client and the X server busy.

Now the screen area is captured with MIT-SHM into a buffer that is
reused while the size and zoom stay the same (XGetSubImage without
SHM), scaled on the client side into a second shared image and sent
with one XShmPutImage per frame, at 30 frames per second. A frame whose
captured pixels, pointer position and zoom did not change is not drawn
again. Near a screen edge the captured area stays inside the screen.

- Zoom: mouse wheel over the view (steps from 1x to 16x, including
  1.25x, 1.5x, 2.5x...), "+" and "-", "0" or the middle button go back
  to 2x; the keys 1-9 still set integer factors; -m accepts fractions.
- Scaling: pixel-perfect (default) or smooth (bilinear), "s" or -smooth.
- Window: freely resizable with a 120x80 minimum; kept above the other
  windows (_NET_WM_STATE_ABOVE) by default, "a" toggles, -noabove.
- WMAGNIFY_STATS=1 prints frames per second and the time spent capturing,
  scaling and drawing.

Measured in Xvfb (1920x1080, Window Maker, 600x400 view at 2x, pointer
moving for 10 s; CPU as a share of one core): before, 4 updates/s,
2.39% wmagnify + 4.88% X server; after, 29 updates/s, 1.20% + 1.38%
(pixel-perfect) and 3.64% + 1.39% (smooth).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
